        "title": "Headline finding",
        "content": "\n**There is no test infrastructure in this project at all.** No `vitest`/`jest` dependency, no config, no `*.test.ts` file, no `__tests__` directory. `package.json` has no `test` script. Every function below is therefore \"untested\" in the literal sense — this isn't a prioritization problem, it's a bootstrapping problem.\n\nNext.js 16's own guide (`node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/02-guides/testing/vitest.md`) recommends **Vitest + React Testing Library**, with one caveat worth keeping in mind for later: *Vitest cannot unit-test `async` Server Components* (all of this app's pages are async Server Components) — those need Playwright/E2E, not Vitest. Route handlers, `lib/`, and `types/` are all synchronous or async-but-not-React, so Vitest handles them fine.\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Recommended setup (not yet done — do this first)",
        "content": "\n```bash\nnpm install -D vitest @vitejs/plugin-react jsdom @testing-library/react @testing-library/dom vite-tsconfig-paths\n```\n\n```ts\n// vitest.config.mts\nimport { defineConfig } from \"vitest/config\";\nimport react from \"@vitejs/plugin-react\";\nimport tsconfigPaths from \"vite-tsconfig-paths\";\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [tsconfigPaths(), react()],\n  test: { environment: \"node\" }, // \"jsdom\" only needed once component tests exist\n});\n```\n\n```json\n// package.json — add\n\"scripts\": { \"test\": \"vitest\" }\n```\n\n`lib/server/*` and `app/api/*` import `server-only` and hit Postgres via `getDb()` / the engine via `fetch`. Those need either a mocked `lib/db` module (`vi.mock(\"@/lib/db\")`) or a disposable test database (Drizzle against a local/Docker Postgres) — I've noted per-file which approach fits.\n\n---\n\n",
