      {
        "title": "`lib/validate-value.ts` — `foldValue` / `checkObservedValue` / `observedValues` (business-critical: this is the web-side mirror of `engine/pipeline/normalize.py`; a drift here breaks the \"must stay in lockstep\" contract)",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// lib/validate-value.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { foldValue, checkObservedValue, observedValues, LEGAL_SUFFIXES } from \"@/lib/validate-value\";\n\ndescribe(\"foldValue\", () => {\n  it(\"lowercases and strips punctuation\", () => {\n    expect(foldValue(\"ACME-INC\")).toBe(foldValue(\"ACME INC\"));\n  });\n  it(\"strips a single legal suffix\", () => {\n    expect(foldValue(\"Trellis Labs Inc\")).toBe(\"trellis labs\");\n  });\n  it(\"strips two stacked suffixes ('Pvt Ltd')\", () => {\n    expect(foldValue(\"Trellis Labs Pvt Ltd\")).toBe(\"trellis labs\");\n  });\n  it(\"prefers the longer suffix match ('pvt ltd' over 'ltd')\", () => {\n    expect(foldValue(\"Foo Pvt Ltd\")).toBe(\"foo\");\n  });\n  it(\"does not strip a suffix that is the entire value\", () => {\n    expect(foldValue(\"Ltd\")).toBe(\"ltd\");\n    expect(foldValue(\"Pvt Ltd\")).toBe(\"pvt ltd\");\n  });\n  it(\"collapses internal whitespace and trims\", () => {\n    expect(foldValue(\"  Acme   Inc.  \")).toBe(\"acme\");\n  });\n  it(\"is idempotent\", () => {\n    const once = foldValue(\"ACME Corp.\");\n    expect(foldValue(once)).toBe(once);\n  });\n  it.each(LEGAL_SUFFIXES)(\"recognizes suffix '%s' at the tail\", (suffix) => {\n    expect(foldValue(`Foo ${suffix}`)).toBe(\"foo\");\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"checkObservedValue\", () => {\n  it(\"rejects an empty/whitespace candidate\", () => {\n    expect(checkObservedValue(\"   \", [\"ACC-1\"])).toEqual({\n      ok: false, reason: \"A value is required.\",\n    });\n  });\n  it(\"rejects when neither side holds a value\", () => {\n    expect(checkObservedValue(\"ACC-1\", [\"\", \"  \"])).toMatchObject({ ok: false });\n  });\n  it(\"accepts an exact match\", () => {\n    expect(checkObservedValue(\"ACC-1\", [\"ACC-1\", \"ACC-2\"])).toEqual({ ok: true, matches: \"ACC-1\" });\n  });\n  it(\"accepts a folded (legal-normalization) match\", () => {\n    expect(checkObservedValue(\"ACME INC\", [\"Acme Inc.\"])).toEqual({ ok: true, matches: \"Acme Inc.\" });\n  });\n  it(\"rejects an invented value not present on either side\", () => {\n    const result = checkObservedValue(\"ACC-9999\", [\"ACC-1\", \"ACC-2\"]);\n    expect(result.ok).toBe(false);\n    if (!result.ok) expect(result.reason).toContain(\"ACC-1 or ACC-2\");\n  });\n  it(\"trims the candidate before comparing\", () => {\n    expect(checkObservedValue(\"  ACC-1  \", [\"ACC-1\"])).toEqual({ ok: true, matches: \"ACC-1\" });\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"observedValues\", () => {\n  it(\"flattens both sides and drops blanks\", () => {\n    expect(observedValues({ a: { values: [\"X\", \"\"] }, b: { values: [\"Y\"] } })).toEqual([\"X\", \"Y\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"returns empty array for an orphan (one side absent)\", () => {\n    expect(observedValues({ a: { values: [] }, b: { values: [\"Y\"] } })).toEqual([\"Y\"]);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`lib/uuid.ts`, `lib/reviewer-label.ts`, `lib/cn.ts` — small but load-bearing",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// lib/uuid.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { isUuid } from \"@/lib/uuid\";\n\ndescribe(\"isUuid\", () => {\n  it(\"accepts a valid v4-shaped uuid\", () => {\n    expect(isUuid(\"a41fc9d2-1234-4abc-8abc-000000000000\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"is case-insensitive\", () => {\n    expect(isUuid(\"A41FC9D2-1234-4ABC-8ABC-000000000000\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects a route param someone typed by hand\", () => {\n    expect(isUuid(\"banana\")).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects an id missing hyphens\", () => {\n    expect(isUuid(\"a41fc9d212344abc8abc000000000000\")).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects empty string\", () => expect(isUuid(\"\")).toBe(false));\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// lib/reviewer-label.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { reviewerLabel } from \"@/lib/reviewer-label\";\n\ndescribe(\"reviewerLabel\", () => {\n  it(\"prefers username\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"j.rao\", primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"fallback\"))\n      .toBe(\"j.rao\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to email when username is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"fallback\"))\n      .toBe(\"j@x.com\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to the passed fallback when user is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel(null, \"user_123\")).toBe(\"user_123\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back when email object exists but emailAddress is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: null } }, \"user_123\"))\n      .toBe(\"user_123\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back when username is an empty string\", () => {\n    // NOTE: `??` treats \"\" as present, not nullish — verify actual behavior here.\n    // If this test fails, it's a real bug: an empty-string username would render as blank\n    // in the audit trail instead of falling back.\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"\", primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"user_123\")).toBe(\"user_123\");\n  });\n});\n```\n\nI flagged that last case deliberately — `user?.username ?? ...` does **not** fall through on `\"\"`, only `null`/`undefined`. If Clerk can ever return an empty-string username, this is a live bug (an audit-log actor rendered as blank). Worth confirming Clerk's contract and adding the guard if not, but that's a correctness question for you to weigh, not something I should silently patch.\n\n```ts\n// lib/cn.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { cn } from \"@/lib/cn\";\n\ndescribe(\"cn\", () => {\n  it(\"merges plain class strings\", () => expect(cn(\"a\", \"b\")).toBe(\"a b\"));\n  it(\"resolves tailwind conflicts, last wins\", () => expect(cn(\"p-2\", \"p-4\")).toBe(\"p-4\"));\n  it(\"drops falsy values\", () => expect(cn(\"a\", false, null, undefined, \"b\")).toBe(\"a b\"));\n  it(\"handles conditional object form\", () => expect(cn({ a: true, b: false })).toBe(\"a\"));\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`lib/api-client.ts` — `postJson` (async, network-facing — needs `fetch` mocking)",
        "content": "\nUntested paths: network failure (`fetch` throws), non-JSON error body, JSON error body missing `.error`, success path, `issues` passthrough.\n\n```ts\n// lib/api-client.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi, afterEach } from \"vitest\";\nimport { postJson } from \"@/lib/api-client\";\n\nafterEach(() => vi.restoreAllMocks());\n\ndescribe(\"postJson\", () => {\n  it(\"returns ok:true with parsed data on 2xx\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(\n      new Response(JSON.stringify({ id: \"1\" }), { status: 201 }),\n    ));\n    const result = await postJson<{ id: string }>(\"/api/sources\", { name: \"x\" });\n    expect(result).toEqual({ ok: true, data: { id: \"1\" } });\n  });\n\n  it(\"surfaces server-supplied error + field issues on 4xx\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(\n      new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: \"Invalid request body.\", issues: { name: \"Required\" } }), { status: 400 }),\n    ));\n    const result = await postJson(\"/api/sources\", {});\n    expect(result).toEqual({\n      ok: false, message: \"Invalid request body.\", issues: { name: \"Required\" },\n    });\n  });\n\n  it(\"falls back to a status-code message when the error body has no `error` field\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(\"{}\", { status: 500 })));\n    const result = await postJson(\"/api/sources\", {});\n    expect(result).toEqual({ ok: false, message: \"Request failed (500).\", issues: undefined });\n  });\n\n  it(\"handles a non-JSON error body without throwing\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(\"<html>502</html>\", { status: 502 })));\n    const result = await postJson(\"/api/sources\", {});\n    expect(result.ok).toBe(false);\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns a friendly message when fetch itself rejects (offline / DNS failure)\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockRejectedValue(new TypeError(\"Failed to fetch\")));\n    const result = await postJson(\"/api/sources\", { name: \"x\" });\n    expect(result).toEqual({\n      ok: false, message: \"Could not reach the server. Check your connection and retry.\",\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "B. `types/` — derived business-rule functions (pure, but encode real policy)",
        "content": "\nThese are easy to overlook because they live in a \"schemas\" file, but several are non-trivial logic, not just Zod definitions.\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "`types/run.ts` — `runStageIndex`, `isTerminalRunStatus`, `isStatKnown`, `failedStage`",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// types/run.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport {\n  isTerminalRunStatus, runStageIndex, isStatKnown, failedStage, RUN_STAGES,\n} from \"@/types/run\";\n\ndescribe(\"isTerminalRunStatus\", () => {\n  it.each(RUN_STAGES.filter((s) => s !== \"complete\"))(\"%s is not terminal\", (status) => {\n    expect(isTerminalRunStatus(status)).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"complete and failed are terminal\", () => {\n    expect(isTerminalRunStatus(\"complete\")).toBe(true);\n    expect(isTerminalRunStatus(\"failed\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"isStatKnown\", () => {\n  it(\"a stat is unknown before its producing stage completes\", () => {\n    expect(isStatKnown(\"matches\", \"queued\")).toBe(false);\n    expect(isStatKnown(\"matches\", \"extracting\")).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"a stat is known once its stage is behind us\", () => {\n    expect(isStatKnown(\"matches\", \"detecting\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"every stat is known once complete, regardless of stage mapping\", () => {\n    expect(isStatKnown(\"tokensUsed\", \"complete\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"every stat is known (not blanked) on a failed run\", () => {\n    // Documented deliberate choice: failed run shows whatever it computed.\n    expect(isStatKnown(\"escalated\", \"failed\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"failedStage\", () => {\n  it(\"parses the trailing stage= convention\", () => {\n    expect(failedStage(\"EngineDispatch: timeout\\nstage=dispatch\")).toBe(\"queued\");\n  });\n  it(\"is case-insensitive on the verb\", () => {\n    expect(failedStage(\"stage=EXTRACT\")).toBe(\"extracting\");\n  });\n  it(\"returns null when the error has no stage= marker\", () => {\n    expect(failedStage(\"Postgres connection refused\")).toBeNull();\n  });\n  it(\"returns null for null input\", () => {\n    expect(failedStage(null)).toBeNull();\n  });\n  it(\"returns null for an unrecognized stage verb\", () => {\n    expect(failedStage(\"stage=teleport\")).toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"runStageIndex\", () => {\n  it(\"returns -1 for failed (not a real stage)\", () => expect(runStageIndex(\"failed\")).toBe(-1));\n  it(\"orders stages as declared\", () => {\n    expect(runStageIndex(\"queued\")).toBeLessThan(runStageIndex(\"complete\"));\n  });\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`types/resolution.ts` — `appliedValue`, `isHumanDecided`",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// types/resolution.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { appliedValue, isHumanDecided } from \"@/types/resolution\";\n\ndescribe(\"appliedValue\", () => {\n  it(\"returns the override value when overridden\", () => {\n    expect(appliedValue({ status: \"overridden\", proposedValue: \"X\", overrideValue: \"Y\" })).toBe(\"Y\");\n  });\n  it(\"returns the proposed value when auto_applied or approved\", () => {\n    expect(appliedValue({ status: \"auto_applied\", proposedValue: \"X\", overrideValue: null })).toBe(\"X\");\n    expect(appliedValue({ status: \"approved\", proposedValue: \"X\", overrideValue: null })).toBe(\"X\");\n  });\n  it(\"returns null for escalated/proposed/rejected — no value ever took effect\", () => {\n    for (const status of [\"escalated\", \"proposed\", \"rejected\"] as const) {\n      expect(appliedValue({ status, proposedValue: \"X\", overrideValue: null })).toBeNull();\n    }\n  });\n  it(\"treats an empty proposedValue as no applied value (falsy guard)\", () => {\n    expect(appliedValue({ status: \"approved\", proposedValue: \"\", overrideValue: null })).toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"isHumanDecided\", () => {\n  it.each([\"approved\", \"rejected\", \"overridden\"] as const)(\"%s counts as decided\", (s) => {\n    expect(isHumanDecided(s)).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it.each([\"proposed\", \"auto_applied\", \"escalated\"] as const)(\"%s does not count as decided\", (s) => {\n    expect(isHumanDecided(s)).toBe(false);\n  });\n});\n```\n\nNote: `types/resolution.ts` defines `appliedValue` with slightly different behavior than `lib/server/export.ts`'s own `appliedValue` (the export one checks `APPLIED_STATUSES.has(status)` against a `Set` and doesn't have the \"empty proposedValue → null\" falsy guard). **These two must agree** — a resolution that shows as \"applied\" in one and not the other is exactly the kind of drift AGENTS.md warns about for the engine/web split. This is worth a dedicated cross-consistency test:\n\n```ts\n// types/resolution.applied-value-parity.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, fc } from \"vitest\"; // or manual cases if no fast-check dep\nimport { appliedValue as typeAppliedValue } from \"@/types/resolution\";\n// export's version is internal (not exported) — consider exporting it for this test,\n// or duplicate its cases here explicitly so a future edit to either file trips a diff.\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`types/rules.ts` — `orderRulesByEvaluation`, `ruleSpecificity`",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// types/rules.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { orderRulesByEvaluation, ruleSpecificity, type SurvivorshipRule } from \"@/types/rules\";\n\nconst rule = (over: Partial<SurvivorshipRule>): SurvivorshipRule => ({\n  id: \"r\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"escalate\", description: \"\", ...over,\n});\n\ndescribe(\"ruleSpecificity\", () => {\n  it(\"wildcard/wildcard is least specific\", () => expect(ruleSpecificity(rule({}))).toBe(0));\n  it(\"field-only beats wildcard/wildcard\", () => {\n    expect(ruleSpecificity(rule({ field: \"name\" }))).toBeGreaterThan(ruleSpecificity(rule({})));\n  });\n  it(\"field+entityType is most specific\", () => {\n    expect(ruleSpecificity(rule({ field: \"name\", entityType: \"account\" })))\n      .toBe(3);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"orderRulesByEvaluation\", () => {\n  it(\"sorts most specific first\", () => {\n    const wide = rule({ id: \"wide\" });\n    const narrow = rule({ id: \"narrow\", field: \"name\", entityType: \"account\" });\n    expect(orderRulesByEvaluation([wide, narrow]).map((r) => r.id)).toEqual([\"narrow\", \"wide\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"preserves author order for equally-specific rules (stable sort, ties on index)\", () => {\n    const a = rule({ id: \"a\", field: \"name\" });\n    const b = rule({ id: \"b\", field: \"phone\" });\n    expect(orderRulesByEvaluation([a, b]).map((r) => r.id)).toEqual([\"a\", \"b\"]);\n    expect(orderRulesByEvaluation([b, a]).map((r) => r.id)).toEqual([\"b\", \"a\"]);\n  });\n  it(\"does not mutate the input array\", () => {\n    const input = [rule({ id: \"a\" }), rule({ id: \"b\", field: \"name\" })];\n    const copy = [...input];\n    orderRulesByEvaluation(input);\n    expect(input).toEqual(copy);\n  });\n});\n```\n\nAlso missing: the `survivorshipRuleSchema.refine` — `prefer_source` requires `preferredSourceId`. This is Zod validation logic, not a plain function, but it's a real branch that needs a direct test:\n\n```ts\ndescribe(\"survivorshipRuleSchema refine\", () => {\n  it(\"rejects prefer_source without preferredSourceId\", () => {\n    const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n      id: \"r\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"prefer_source\", description: \"\",\n    });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"accepts prefer_source with preferredSourceId\", () => {\n    const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n      id: \"r\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"prefer_source\",\n      preferredSourceId: \"src-1\", description: \"\",\n    });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`types/queue.ts` — `conflictFieldLabel`, `severityReason`",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// types/queue.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { conflictFieldLabel, severityReason } from \"@/types/queue\";\n\ndescribe(\"conflictFieldLabel\", () => {\n  it(\"returns the field name for a regular disagreement\", () => {\n    expect(conflictFieldLabel({ field: \"name\", conflictClass: \"attribute\", a: { values: [\"x\"], present: true }, b: { values: [\"y\"], present: true } }))\n      .toBe(\"name\");\n  });\n  it(\"returns a fixed label for orphan, ignoring field\", () => {\n    expect(conflictFieldLabel({ field: \"irrelevant\", conflictClass: \"orphan\", a: { values: [], present: false }, b: { values: [\"y\"], present: true } }))\n      .toBe(\"entity absent\");\n  });\n  it(\"shows N → M collapse for duplicates with unequal counts\", () => {\n    expect(conflictFieldLabel({ field: \"x\", conflictClass: \"duplicate\", a: { values: [\"1\", \"2\"], present: true }, b: { values: [\"1\"], present: true } }))\n      .toBe(\"2 → 1\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to 'duplicate record' when counts are equal or one side is empty\", () => {\n    expect(conflictFieldLabel({ field: \"x\", conflictClass: \"duplicate\", a: { values: [\"1\", \"2\"], present: true }, b: { values: [], present: false } }))\n      .toBe(\"duplicate record\");\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"severityReason\", () => {\n  it(\"explains orphan with children differently from without\", () => {\n    expect(severityReason({ conflictClass: \"orphan\", severity: 4, childCount: 3 })).toContain(\"3 children\");\n    expect(severityReason({ conflictClass: \"orphan\", severity: 3, childCount: 1 })).toContain(\"1 child\"); // singular\n    expect(severityReason({ conflictClass: \"orphan\", severity: 3, childCount: 0 })).toContain(\"no children\");\n  });\n  it(\"distinguishes severity 3 vs 2 for plain attribute conflicts\", () => {\n    expect(severityReason({ conflictClass: \"attribute\", severity: 3, childCount: 0 })).toContain(\"owns or is credited\");\n    expect(severityReason({ conflictClass: \"attribute\", severity: 2, childCount: 0 })).toContain(\"non-rollup\");\n  });\n});\n```\n\nAnd `types/queue.ts`'s `queueQuerySchema` has a hand-rolled `severities` transform (`\"4,3\"` → `[4, 3]`, silently dropping invalid entries) that's exactly the kind of string-parsing logic that breaks silently:\n\n```ts\ndescribe(\"queueQuerySchema severities parsing\", () => {\n  it(\"parses a comma-separated list\", () => {\n    expect(queueQuerySchema.parse({ severities: \"4,3\" }).severities).toEqual([4, 3]);\n  });\n  it(\"drops out-of-range or non-numeric entries instead of throwing\", () => {\n    expect(queueQuerySchema.parse({ severities: \"4,9,abc,2\" }).severities).toEqual([4, 2]);\n  });\n  it(\"defaults to an empty array when the param is absent\", () => {\n    expect(queueQuerySchema.parse({}).severities).toEqual([]);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "C. `lib/server/` — DB & engine-HTTP integration layer",
        "content": "\nThese need `vi.mock(\"@/lib/db\")` (unit-style, mocking the Drizzle query builder) or a real test Postgres via Drizzle migrations. Given this is an internal audit-critical tool, I'd lean toward a real test DB for `runs.ts`/`export.ts`/`rulesets.ts` — mocking Drizzle's fluent builder tends to produce brittle tests that don't actually catch join/query bugs.\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "`lib/server/engine.ts` — fully untestable-by-design right now (no seam for injecting `fetch`), but the logic is the most error-prone in the whole server layer",
        "content": "\nUntested: `startRun` success, engine rejecting with non-2xx, engine timing out (`AbortSignal.timeout`), engine returning malformed JSON, engine returning wrong-shaped JSON (schema mismatch), `fetchEngineStatus` returning `null` on every failure mode, `describe()`'s `TimeoutError` branch, missing `ENGINE_URL` env var.\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/engine.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi, beforeEach, afterEach } from \"vitest\";\n\nconst ORIGINAL_ENV = process.env.ENGINE_URL;\nbeforeEach(() => { process.env.ENGINE_URL = \"http://engine.test\"; vi.resetModules(); });\nafterEach(() => { process.env.ENGINE_URL = ORIGINAL_ENV; vi.restoreAllMocks(); });\n\ndescribe(\"startRun\", () => {\n  it(\"throws EngineUnreachableError when ENGINE_URL is unset\", async () => {\n    delete process.env.ENGINE_URL;\n    const { startRun, EngineUnreachableError } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(startRun(\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\")).rejects.toThrow(EngineUnreachableError);\n  });\n\n  it(\"resolves when the engine accepts with the expected contract\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(\n      new Response(JSON.stringify({ runId: \"r1\", accepted: true }), { status: 200 }),\n    ));\n    const { startRun } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(startRun(\"r1\")).resolves.toBeUndefined();\n  });\n\n  it(\"throws EngineUnreachableError on non-2xx, with the response body in the message\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(\"bad ruleset id\", { status: 422 })));\n    const { startRun, EngineUnreachableError } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(startRun(\"r1\")).rejects.toThrow(/422/);\n  });\n\n  it(\"throws EngineUnreachableError when the engine 200s with the wrong shape (contract break)\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(JSON.stringify({ ok: true }), { status: 200 })));\n    const { startRun, EngineUnreachableError } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(startRun(\"r1\")).rejects.toThrow(EngineUnreachableError);\n  });\n\n  it(\"wraps a network-level failure (connection refused)\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockRejectedValue(new TypeError(\"fetch failed\")));\n    const { startRun } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(startRun(\"r1\")).rejects.toThrow(/fetch failed/);\n  });\n\n  it(\"reports a timeout distinctly from other network errors\", async () => {\n    const timeoutError = new DOMException(\"The operation was aborted\", \"TimeoutError\");\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockRejectedValue(timeoutError));\n    const { startRun } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(startRun(\"r1\")).rejects.toThrow(/no response within 8s/);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"fetchEngineStatus\", () => {\n  it(\"returns the parsed status on success\", async () => {\n    const body = { runId: \"r1\", status: \"matching\", stats: EMPTY_RUN_STATS_LIKE, error: null };\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(JSON.stringify(body), { status: 200 })));\n    const { fetchEngineStatus } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(\"r1\")).resolves.toMatchObject({ status: \"matching\" });\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns null (never throws) on a non-2xx response\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(\"\", { status: 503 })));\n    const { fetchEngineStatus } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(\"r1\")).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns null on malformed JSON\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(\"not json\", { status: 200 })));\n    const { fetchEngineStatus } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(\"r1\")).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns null when the response doesn't match engineRunStatusSchema\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(\n      new Response(JSON.stringify({ runId: \"r1\" /* missing fields */ }), { status: 200 }),\n    ));\n    const { fetchEngineStatus } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(\"r1\")).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns null when fetch throws (network down)\", async () => {\n    vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockRejectedValue(new Error(\"ECONNREFUSED\")));\n    const { fetchEngineStatus } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    await expect(fetchEngineStatus(\"r1\")).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n```\n\nThis is the single highest-value file to cover: `startRun` failing silently or `fetchEngineStatus` throwing instead of returning `null` would directly break the \"a run stuck in queued with nothing behind it is the worst outcome\" guarantee the code comments call out.\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`lib/server/export.ts` — `decisionsToCsv`, `applyDecisions`, `appliedValue` (module-private but exercised transitively), `buildRunExport`",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// lib/server/export.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { decisionsToCsv, type DecisionRow } from \"@/lib/server/export\";\n\nconst decision = (over: Partial<DecisionRow>): DecisionRow => ({\n  conflictId: \"c1\", externalId: \"ACC-1\", entityName: \"Acme\", entityType: \"account\",\n  field: \"name\", valueA: \"Acme\", valueB: \"ACME\", conflictClass: \"cosmetic\", severity: 1,\n  detectedBy: \"rule\", status: null, proposedValue: null, overrideValue: null,\n  appliedValue: null, appliedRuleId: null, confidence: null, rationale: null,\n  reviewedBy: null, reviewedAt: null, ...over,\n});\n\ndescribe(\"decisionsToCsv\", () => {\n  it(\"emits a header row matching DECISION_COLUMNS\", () => {\n    const csv = decisionsToCsv([]);\n    expect(csv.split(\"\\r\\n\")[0]).toBe(\n      \"conflictId,externalId,entityName,entityType,field,valueA,valueB,conflictClass,severity,detectedBy,status,proposedValue,overrideValue,appliedValue,appliedRuleId,confidence,rationale,reviewedBy,reviewedAt\",\n    );\n  });\n  it(\"renders null as an empty cell, not the string 'null'\", () => {\n    const csv = decisionsToCsv([decision({})]);\n    expect(csv).not.toContain(\"null\");\n  });\n  it(\"quotes and escapes a value containing a comma\", () => {\n    const csv = decisionsToCsv([decision({ valueA: \"Acme, Inc.\" })]);\n    expect(csv).toContain('\"Acme, Inc.\"');\n  });\n  it(\"escapes an embedded double quote by doubling it\", () => {\n    const csv = decisionsToCsv([decision({ rationale: 'She said \"yes\"' })]);\n    expect(csv).toContain('\"She said \"\"yes\"\"\"');\n  });\n  it(\"quotes a value containing a newline or carriage return\", () => {\n    const csv = decisionsToCsv([decision({ rationale: \"line1\\nline2\" })]);\n    expect(csv).toContain('\"line1\\nline2\"');\n  });\n  it(\"ends the file with a trailing CRLF\", () => {\n    expect(decisionsToCsv([decision({})]).endsWith(\"\\r\\n\")).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it(\"returns just the header (no trailing blank row) for zero decisions\", () => {\n    expect(decisionsToCsv([]).split(\"\\r\\n\")).toEqual([expect.any(String), \"\"]);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n`applyDecisions` and the private `appliedValue` in this file aren't exported — worth exporting them (or testing indirectly through `buildRunExport` against a seeded test DB) since they carry real merge logic: a `parentExternalId` decision must override the raw side-A row, and `reparented` must be `true` only when the resolved value actually differs from the original. That's a distinct scenario from \"resolved value happens to equal the original\" (should be `reparented: false`) — currently untested and easy to get backwards.\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/export.apply-decisions.test.ts (requires exporting applyDecisions, or an integration test via buildRunExport)\nit(\"marks reparented=true only when the applied parent differs from the original\", () => {\n  // account originally under P1; a decision applies P2 -> reparented true\n  // account originally under P1; a decision applies P1 (no-op) -> reparented false\n  // account has no decision at all -> keeps original parent, reparented false\n});\nit(\"leaves parentExternalId untouched when the resolution field isn't parentExternalId\", () => {\n  // a 'name' field decision must not affect hierarchy.parentExternalId\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`lib/server/rulesets.ts` — `createRulesetVersion` (transaction + advisory lock — the concurrency-sensitive one)",
        "content": "\nThis needs a real test database — mocking `db.transaction`/`pg_advisory_xact_lock` would test nothing. Integration tests:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/rulesets.integration.test.ts (against a disposable test Postgres)\ndescribe(\"createRulesetVersion\", () => {\n  it(\"starts a new policy name at version 1\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"increments version for an existing name\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"deactivates the previously active ruleset when a new one is created\", async () => {\n    // Assert exactly one row has isActive=true after two saves of different names.\n  });\n  it(\"two concurrent creates for the same name never produce a duplicate version number\", async () => {\n    // Fire createRulesetVersion({name: \"x\", ...}) twice via Promise.all and assert\n    // the resulting versions are {1,2}, not {1,1} — this is the whole reason the\n    // advisory lock exists, and it's currently the only test that would catch a\n    // regression if someone \"simplified\" the transaction.\n    const [a, b] = await Promise.all([\n      createRulesetVersion({ name: \"concurrent-policy\", rules: [minimalRule] }),\n      createRulesetVersion({ name: \"concurrent-policy\", rules: [minimalRule] }),\n    ]);\n    expect(new Set([a.version, b.version]).size).toBe(2);\n  });\n  it(\"leaves an unrelated policy's active flag untouched\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n});\n```\n\nThat concurrency test is the most important single test missing from this codebase — it's the only thing that actually exercises the advisory-lock guarantee the code comment promises.\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`lib/server/runs.ts`, `lib/server/sources.ts`, `lib/server/audit.ts`, `lib/server/auth.ts`",
        "content": "\nSame DB-integration shape. Key untested behaviors, one skeleton representative of the pattern (repeat per function):\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/runs.integration.test.ts\ndescribe(\"failUndispatchedRun\", () => {\n  it(\"marks a queued run failed and records the error\", async () => { /* seed a queued run, call, assert row */ });\n  it(\"does NOT overwrite a run the engine has already advanced past 'queued'\", async () => {\n    // seed a run with status \"matching\", call failUndispatchedRun, assert status is unchanged.\n    // This is the guard the code comment calls out explicitly — currently unverified.\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"getSeverityBreakdown\", () => {\n  it(\"returns EMPTY_SEVERITY_BREAKDOWN for a run with no conflicts yet\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"silently drops a severity value outside 1..4 rather than widening the histogram\", async () => {\n    // insert a conflict row with an out-of-range severity (simulating an engine bug)\n    // and assert it doesn't appear/crash rather than trusting the DB constraint alone.\n  });\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/auth.test.ts — mock @clerk/nextjs/server\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi } from \"vitest\";\n\nvi.mock(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\", () => ({\n  auth: vi.fn(),\n  currentUser: vi.fn(),\n}));\n\ndescribe(\"requireReviewer\", () => {\n  it(\"throws when there is no authenticated session (matcher/handler disagreement)\", async () => {\n    const { auth } = await import(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\");\n    vi.mocked(auth).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: false, userId: null } as any);\n    const { requireReviewer } = await import(\"@/lib/server/auth\");\n    await expect(requireReviewer()).rejects.toThrow(/No authenticated reviewer/);\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns userId + resolved label when authenticated\", async () => {\n    const { auth, currentUser } = await import(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\");\n    vi.mocked(auth).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: true, userId: \"user_1\" } as any);\n    vi.mocked(currentUser).mockResolvedValue({ username: \"j.rao\" } as any);\n    const { requireReviewer } = await import(\"@/lib/server/auth\");\n    await expect(requireReviewer()).resolves.toEqual({ userId: \"user_1\", label: \"j.rao\" });\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"getReviewer\", () => {\n  it(\"returns null when signed out, without throwing\", async () => {\n    const { auth } = await import(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\");\n    vi.mocked(auth).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: false } as any);\n    const { getReviewer } = await import(\"@/lib/server/auth\");\n    await expect(getReviewer()).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "D. `app/api/*` route handlers — integration tests (currently zero)",
        "content": "\nRoute handlers in Next 16 App Router can be tested by importing the exported `GET`/`POST` and calling them directly with a `Request`, mocking the underlying `lib/server/*` modules. This is the layer with the most business logic per line and the most missing edge-case coverage.\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "`app/api/_lib/respond.ts` — `parseJson`",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// app/api/_lib/respond.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { parseJson, badRequest, notFound } from \"@/app/api/_lib/respond\";\n\ndescribe(\"parseJson\", () => {\n  const schema = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1) });\n\n  it(\"returns ok:true with typed data on valid body\", async () => {\n    const req = new Request(\"http://x\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ name: \"a\" }) });\n    const result = await parseJson(req, schema);\n    expect(result).toEqual({ ok: true, data: { name: \"a\" } });\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns a 400 Response when the body is not JSON at all\", async () => {\n    const req = new Request(\"http://x\", { method: \"POST\", body: \"not json\" });\n    const result = await parseJson(req, schema);\n    expect(result.ok).toBe(false);\n    if (!result.ok) expect(result.response.status).toBe(400);\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns 400 with field-addressable issues when schema validation fails\", async () => {\n    const req = new Request(\"http://x\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ name: \"\" }) });\n    const result = await parseJson(req, schema);\n    if (!result.ok) {\n      const body = await result.response.json();\n      expect(body.issues).toHaveProperty(\"name\");\n    }\n  });\n\n  it(\"uses '_' as the key for a top-level (pathless) issue\", async () => {\n    const req = new Request(\"http://x\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify(\"a string, not an object\") });\n    const result = await parseJson(req, schema);\n    if (!result.ok) {\n      const body = await result.response.json();\n      expect(Object.keys(body.issues)).toContain(\"_\");\n    }\n  });\n\n  it(\"joins nested paths with dots (e.g. config.objects)\", async () => {\n    const nested = z.object({ config: z.object({ objects: z.array(z.string()).min(1) }) });\n    const req = new Request(\"http://x\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ config: { objects: [] } }) });\n    const result = await parseJson(req, nested);\n    if (!result.ok) {\n      const body = await result.response.json();\n      expect(body.issues).toHaveProperty(\"config.objects\");\n    }\n  });\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`app/api/runs/route.ts` — `POST` (the richest handler: validation, self-reconciliation guard, FK pre-check, audit write, engine dispatch failure path)",
        "content": "\n```ts\n// app/api/runs/route.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it, vi, beforeEach } from \"vitest\";\n\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/auth\", () => ({ requireReviewer: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/audit\", () => ({ appendAudit: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/engine\", async (importOriginal) => {\n  const actual = await importOriginal<typeof import(\"@/lib/server/engine\")>();\n  return { ...actual, startRun: vi.fn() };\n});\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/runs\", () => ({\n  createRun: vi.fn(), failUndispatchedRun: vi.fn(), listRuns: vi.fn(),\n}));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/rulesets\", () => ({ getRuleset: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/sources\", () => ({ findSourceIds: vi.fn() }));\n\nconst body = (b: object) => new Request(\"http://x/api/runs\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify(b) });\nconst uuid = (n: number) => `00000000-0000-0000-0000-${String(n).padStart(12, \"0\")}`;\n\ndescribe(\"POST /api/runs\", () => {\n  it(\"400s when sourceAId equals sourceBId\", async () => {\n    const { POST } = await import(\"@/app/api/runs/route\");\n    const res = await POST(body({ sourceAId: uuid(1), sourceBId: uuid(1), rulesetId: uuid(2) }));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(400);\n    expect(await res.json()).toMatchObject({ error: expect.stringContaining(\"must be different\") });\n  });\n\n  it(\"400s with a specific message when sourceAId doesn't exist (not a raw FK 500)\", async () => {\n    const { findSourceIds } = await import(\"@/lib/server/sources\");\n    const { getRuleset } = await import(\"@/lib/server/rulesets\");\n    vi.mocked(findSourceIds).mockResolvedValue(new Set([uuid(2)])); // only B exists\n    vi.mocked(getRuleset).mockResolvedValue({ id: uuid(3) } as any);\n    const { POST } = await import(\"@/app/api/runs/route\");\n    const res = await POST(body({ sourceAId: uuid(1), sourceBId: uuid(2), rulesetId: uuid(3) }));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(400);\n    expect(await res.json()).toMatchObject({ error: \"Source A no longer exists.\" });\n  });\n\n  it(\"400s when the ruleset id no longer exists\", async () => {\n    const { findSourceIds } = await import(\"@/lib/server/sources\");\n    const { getRuleset } = await import(\"@/lib/server/rulesets\");\n    vi.mocked(findSourceIds).mockResolvedValue(new Set([uuid(1), uuid(2)]));\n    vi.mocked(getRuleset).mockResolvedValue(null);\n    const { POST } = await import(\"@/app/api/runs/route\");\n    const res = await POST(body({ sourceAId: uuid(1), sourceBId: uuid(2), rulesetId: uuid(3) }));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(400);\n  });\n\n  it(\"creates the run, dispatches to the engine, and returns 201 with runId on success\", async () => {\n    const { findSourceIds } = await import(\"@/lib/server/sources\");\n    const { getRuleset } = await import(\"@/lib/server/rulesets\");\n    const { requireReviewer } = await import(\"@/lib/server/auth\");\n    const { createRun } = await import(\"@/lib/server/runs\");\n    const { startRun } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    vi.mocked(findSourceIds).mockResolvedValue(new Set([uuid(1), uuid(2)]));\n    vi.mocked(getRuleset).mockResolvedValue({ id: uuid(3), name: \"policy\", version: 1 } as any);\n    vi.mocked(requireReviewer).mockResolvedValue({ userId: \"u1\", label: \"j.rao\" });\n    vi.mocked(createRun).mockResolvedValue({ id: \"run-1\" } as any);\n    vi.mocked(startRun).mockResolvedValue(undefined);\n\n    const { POST } = await import(\"@/app/api/runs/route\");\n    const res = await POST(body({ sourceAId: uuid(1), sourceBId: uuid(2), rulesetId: uuid(3) }));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(201);\n    expect(await res.json()).toEqual({ runId: \"run-1\" });\n  });\n\n  it(\"still returns 201 when the engine is unreachable, but marks the run failed and audits it (wireframe 1h)\", async () => {\n    const { findSourceIds } = await import(\"@/lib/server/sources\");\n    const { getRuleset } = await import(\"@/lib/server/rulesets\");\n    const { requireReviewer } = await import(\"@/lib/server/auth\");\n    const { createRun, failUndispatchedRun } = await import(\"@/lib/server/runs\");\n    const { startRun, EngineUnreachableError } = await import(\"@/lib/server/engine\");\n    const { appendAudit } = await import(\"@/lib/server/audit\");\n    vi.mocked(findSourceIds).mockResolvedValue(new Set([uuid(1), uuid(2)]));\n    vi.mocked(getRuleset).mockResolvedValue({ id: uuid(3), name: \"policy\", version: 1 } as any);\n    vi.mocked(requireReviewer).mockResolvedValue({ userId: \"u1\", label: \"j.rao\" });\n    vi.mocked(createRun).mockResolvedValue({ id: \"run-1\" } as any);\n    vi.mocked(startRun).mockRejectedValue(new EngineUnreachableError(\"connection refused\"));\n\n    const { POST } = await import(\"@/app/api/runs/route\");\n    const res = await POST(body({ sourceAId: uuid(1), sourceBId: uuid(2), rulesetId: uuid(3) }));\n\n    expect(res.status).toBe(201); // NOT 500 — this is the behavior the code comment calls out explicitly\n    expect(failUndispatchedRun).toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"run-1\", expect.stringContaining(\"stage=dispatch\"));\n    expect(appendAudit).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.objectContaining({ action: \"run_failed\" }));\n  });\n\n  it(\"re-throws a non-EngineUnreachableError instead of swallowing it as a dispatch failure\", async () => {\n    // startRun throwing e.g. a TypeError (programmer bug) must NOT be recorded as\n    // a normal \"engine down\" failure — currently unverified.\n  });\n\n  it(\"resolves the audit actor from the session, never from the request body\", async () => {\n    // POST a body containing an extra `actor`/`reviewedBy`-like field (if the schema\n    // ever loosens) and assert appendAudit's actor still comes from requireReviewer().\n  });\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "`app/api/runs/[runId]/route.ts`, `.../status/route.ts`, `.../export/route.ts`, `app/api/sources/route.ts`, `app/api/rulesets/route.ts`",
        "content": "\nSame pattern; representative skeleton + the specific gaps for each:\n\n```ts\n// app/api/runs/[runId]/route.test.ts\ndescribe(\"GET /api/runs/:runId\", () => {\n  it(\"404s for a non-UUID param without touching the DB\", async () => {\n    const { getRunDetail } = await import(\"@/lib/server/runs\");\n    const { GET } = await import(\"@/app/api/runs/[runId]/route\");\n    const res = await GET(new Request(\"http://x\"), { params: Promise.resolve({ runId: \"banana\" }) });\n    expect(res.status).toBe(404);\n    expect(getRunDetail).not.toHaveBeenCalled();\n  });\n  it(\"404s for a well-formed UUID that doesn't exist\", async () => { /* getRunDetail resolves null */ });\n  it(\"200s with the joined run detail for a real run\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// app/api/runs/[runId]/status/route.test.ts — the most behaviorally interesting route\ndescribe(\"GET /api/runs/:runId/status\", () => {\n  it(\"skips the engine entirely for a terminal run (complete/failed)\", async () => {\n    // seed getRun -> status: \"complete\"; assert fetchEngineStatus was never called\n  });\n  it(\"prefers the live engine status for a non-terminal run\", async () => {\n    // getRun -> \"matching\"; fetchEngineStatus -> live payload; assert live payload returned\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to the DB row when the engine is unreachable mid-run\", async () => {\n    // getRun -> \"matching\"; fetchEngineStatus -> null; assert fromRow shape returned, not an error\n  });\n  it(\"always sets cache-control: no-store (this is the one polled surface)\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"404s for an unknown runId\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// app/api/runs/[runId]/export/route.test.ts\ndescribe(\"GET /api/runs/:runId/export\", () => {\n  it(\"defaults to json when ?format is omitted\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"400s for an unsupported format value\", async () => {\n    const res = await GET(new Request(\"http://x?format=xml\"), ctx);\n    expect(res.status).toBe(400);\n  });\n  it(\"sets a CSV content-type and content-disposition for format=csv\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"sets a JSON content-type and content-disposition for format=json\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"both formats set cache-control: no-store\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"404s before calling requireReviewer for an unknown run (auth work isn't wasted on a dead end)\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"records the actual reviewer identity as exportedBy, never a client-suppliable value\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// app/api/sources/route.test.ts\ndescribe(\"POST /api/sources\", () => {\n  it(\"400s when config.objects is empty (min(1) on the schema)\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"400s when kind is not one of the enum values\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"rejects (or silently ignores?) a body carrying a `credentials`/`password`-like field — verify it's actually impossible, not just undocumented\", async () => {\n    // createSourceSchema has no such field, so Zod's default strip behavior applies.\n    // Test that a body with an extra secret-shaped field still round-trips to a row\n    // with no trace of it, since this is a stated security invariant (AGENTS.md).\n  });\n  it(\"201s with the created source, including a server-assigned id and createdAt\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// app/api/rulesets/route.test.ts\ndescribe(\"POST /api/rulesets\", () => {\n  it(\"400s when rules is an empty array (min(1))\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"400s when a rule uses strategy=prefer_source without preferredSourceId (schema refine bubbling through the route)\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n  it(\"201s and returns the new version, marked active\", async () => { /* ... */ });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "E. `proxy.ts` — auth middleware (currently zero coverage, and it's the whole security boundary)",
        "content": "\nClerk middleware is awkward to unit test directly, but the route matcher logic (`isPublicRoute`) is exactly the kind of regex/matcher config that silently drifts as routes are added. At minimum:\n\n```ts\n// proxy.test.ts\nimport { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { createRouteMatcher } from \"@clerk/nextjs/server\";\n\n// Re-derive (or export) the matcher from proxy.ts so route-list drift is caught directly.\nconst isPublicRoute = createRouteMatcher([\"/\", \"/sign-in(.*)\", \"/sign-up(.*)\"]);\n\ndescribe(\"public route matcher\", () => {\n  it.each([\"/\", \"/sign-in\", \"/sign-in/factor-one\", \"/sign-up\"])(\"%s is public\", (path) => {\n    expect(isPublicRoute(new Request(`http://x${path}`))).toBe(true);\n  });\n  it.each([\"/runs\", \"/runs/new\", \"/api/runs\", \"/api/sources\", \"/sources\"])(\"%s requires auth\", (path) => {\n    expect(isPublicRoute(new Request(`http://x${path}`))).toBe(false);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n**Integration test (E2E, not Vitest — needs a real Next server + Clerk test mode):** an unauthenticated request to `/api/runs` (POST and GET) actually returns a redirect/401 rather than reaching the handler. This is the one test I'd consider non-optional before calling auth \"done\" — everything in `lib/server/auth.ts` assumes `proxy.ts` already gated the request, and that assumption is currently unverified end-to-end.\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "F. A gap that isn't a test gap — it's a coverage gap in the API surface itself",
        "content": "\n`types/resolution.ts` fully defines `reviewActionSchema` (approve/reject/override) with detailed comments about `PATCH /api/conflicts/:conflictId/resolution`, and `lib/validate-value.ts` exists specifically to back the override path. **But no such route exists under `app/api/`.** Either it's genuinely not built yet, or it lives somewhere I haven't found — worth confirming, because right now the override flow (wireframe 1l) has a fully-specified contract and validation logic with nothing to call it. If it's pending, flag it as a build gap; once it exists, it needs the same route-handler test treatment as section D, plus:\n\n```ts\n// app/api/conflicts/[conflictId]/resolution/route.test.ts (once this route exists)\ndescribe(\"PATCH /api/conflicts/:conflictId/resolution\", () => {\n  it(\"approve: sets status=approved, reviewedBy from session, appends human_approved to audit_log\", async () => {});\n  it(\"reject: sets status=rejected, appends human_rejected\", async () => {});\n  it(\"override: runs checkObservedValue server-side and 400s on an invented value\", async () => {\n    // This is the critical path — a reviewer typing a value neither system holds\n    // must be rejected here even if the client-side validator was bypassed.\n  });\n  it(\"override: accepts a legally-normalized value not byte-identical to either observed value\", async () => {});\n  it(\"rejects reviewedBy if a client attempts to supply it in the body (schema has no such field, but verify)\", async () => {});\n  it(\"every action appends exactly one audit_log row, never zero, never two\", async () => {});\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Priority punch list",
        "content": "1. **Bootstrap Vitest** (config above) — nothing else is possible without it.\n2. **`lib/server/engine.ts`** — highest risk-to-effort ratio; a silent bug here produces exactly the \"run stuck in queued\" failure mode the code comments say must never happen.\n3. **`lib/validate-value.ts`** — must stay in lockstep with `engine/pipeline/normalize.py`; a unit test suite here is cheap insurance against silent drift between the two languages.\n4. **`app/api/runs/route.ts` `POST`** — the richest handler, most edge cases, and the dispatch-failure path is load-bearing for a documented UX state (wireframe 1h).\n5. **`lib/server/rulesets.ts` concurrency test** — the only thing that verifies the advisory-lock guarantee actually holds under concurrent writes.\n6. Everything in section A (`lib/`) — trivial to write, zero mocking, and currently 100% uncovered.\n7. Confirm whether the conflict-resolution PATCH route (section F) is a build gap or a discovery gap on my part.\n\nI did not create any test files — everything above is a skeleton for you to drop in once Vitest is wired up. Want me to go ahead and scaffold the Vitest config and write out the `lib/` unit tests (section A), since those need no mocking and would be the fastest way to get from zero to non-zero coverage?",
